
{"id":"f8c8affa-52bb-4976-b374-3713009700e7","object":"chat.completion","created":1784801655,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to generate a Python function `solve(case)` that implements ddmin with unresolved semantics. The task is based on the given fixture. The function must return a JSON-serializable object with specific fields. The input case has items, required_failure_items, unresolved_without. The goal is to find a minimal failure-inducing subset (ordered configuration) using ddmin algorithm that accounts for UNRESOLVED outcomes.\n\nWe need to understand the semantics: There is an oracle that given a subset (list of items) returns PASS, FAIL, or UNRESOLVED. The initial full configuration must produce FAIL. The algorithm: start with full set, granularity=1. Partition into subsets of size granularity. Test subsets first; if any subset yields FAIL, then reduce to that subset and reset granularity to 1? Actually ddmin algorithm: test subsets, if any subset fails, then reduce to that subset and continue with granularity=1. If none fail, test complements (the current set minus each subset). If a complement fails, reduce to that complement (i.e., keep the complement) and double granularity? Wait: Standard ddmin algorithm: Initially test the whole set (must be FAIL). Then set granularity n=2? Actually standard ddmin: split into n subsets, test each subset; if any subset fails, set that subset as new configuration and set n=2? No, the algorithm from Zeller: ddmin(C) where C is set of items. The algorithm: if |C|==1, return C. Otherwise, split C into two halves (or n subsets), test each half; if any half fails, recursively ddmin on that half. If none fail, test complements; if any complement fails, recursively ddmin on that complement. If none, then double the number of subsets? Wait, Zeller's algorithm uses decreasing granularity: start with n=2, then if no reduction, increase n to min(2n, |C|). Actually the standard algorithm: split into n subsets, test each subset; if any fails, then that becomes new C and set n=2; else test complements; if any complement fails, set that complement as new C and set n=2; else increase n to min(2n, |C|). But the problem description says: \"Partition at the current granularity and test subsets before complements. Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not. Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" That is consistent: granularity is the number of subsets. Initially granularity = 2? Or 1? The description: \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" That suggests initial granularity is maybe 2? But it says \"start with current granularity\". I need to interpret carefully.\n\nThe problem says: \"Require the initial configuration to produce FAIL.\" Then \"Partition at the current granularity and test subsets before complements. Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not. Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\"\n\nSo algorithm: Let current configuration S (initially all items). Let granularity g = 1? Or 2? Typically ddmin uses g=2 initially. The phrase \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" After a reduction (when a subset or complement is FAIL), we decrease granularity, meaning set g=2? That would be decreasing from current g? But if we double after non-reduction, then increasing g. So initial g should be small. Possibly initial g=2. But let's examine: \"Decrease granularity after reduction\" could mean set g to 1? But then \"double it up to current size\" meaning if no reduction, double g until it reaches the size of current configuration. That matches the standard: after reduction, set g=2 again. So initial g=2. Alternatively, some implementations start with g=1 (testing each individual item as a subset). But the description says \"granularity\" is number of subsets. So initial partitioning: if g=1, that would be one subset equal to entire set, which is already tested as FAIL. So that would not progress. So g=2 is reasonable.\n\nI'll adopt: start with config = full list of items. granularity = 2. While len(config) > 1 and granularity <= len(config):\n   partition config into granularity subsets (as evenly as possible). For each subset in order:\n       test subset. If FAIL, set config = subset, granularity = 2, break (restart loop).\n   If no subset FAIL:\n       For each subset in order (same subsets? complements? The complements of each subset? Standard ddmin tests complements: config - subset). So we test each complement (i.e., config without that subset). If complement fails, set config = complement, granularity = 2, break.\n   If no complement FAIL:\n       granularity = min(granularity*2, len(config))\nAt end, return config (list of items). Also need to count number of tests performed.\n\nBut there is also UNRESOLVED semantics: \"Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not.\" So if test returns UNRESOLVED, treat like PASS (i.e., not a reduction). So we ignore UNRESOLVED for reduction.\n\nAlso we need an oracle function that given a list of items, returns outcome based on the case payload. The payload has required_failure_items (items that must be present for FAIL) and unresolved_without (items whose absence makes the test UNRESOLVED? Actually \"unresolved_without\" list: if any of those items is missing from the configuration, then outcome is UNRESOLVED? Or if the configuration does not include those items? The description: \"unresolved_without\" - likely if the configuration lacks any of these items, then outcome is UNRESOLVED. Also required_failure_items: all must be present for FAIL? Probably the outcome is FAIL if and only if all required_failure_items are present in the configuration, and additionally no items from unresolved_without are missing? Actually standard semantics: The test fails if all required_failure_items are present. If any of unresolved_without is missing, outcome is UNRESOLVED. If neither condition, outcome is PASS. But careful: The problem statement: \"unresolved_without: []\" in first case. So for first case, no unresolved items, so only FAIL if required_failure_items are all present, else PASS. For second case: unresolved_without: [\"d0\"] means if d0 is missing, outcome is UNRESOLVED. So we need to implement oracle deterministically.\n\nThus oracle: Given config (list of items), check if any item in unresolved_without is not in config -> return UNRESOLVED. Else if all required_failure_items are in config -> return FAIL. Else return PASS.\n\nNote: The initial full configuration must produce FAIL. So we must verify that the full list (all items) contains all required_failure_items and there are no missing unresolved_without (since full config has all items). That ensures initial FAIL.\n\nNow, the function must return a JSON object with fields: one_minimal_failure (the minimal subset that fails), test_count (number of oracle calls), terminal_outcome (the outcome of that minimal subset? Should be FAIL). Also maybe we need to include something like \"one_minimal_result\"? The spec says: \"Return the 1-minimal failure, test count, and terminal outcome.\" So likely output structure: { \"one_minimal_failure\": list, \"test_count\": int, \"terminal_outcome\": \"FAIL\" }.\n\nCheck \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" So we need to use those field names.\n\nAlso we must raise ValueError if case is structurally invalid. For example, if initial configuration does not produce FAIL, or if required_failure_items not all present in items, or unresolved_without not subset of items. But the fixture ensures valid, but we should validate.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Define `solve(case)` that takes a dict with keys: \"items\", \"required_failure_items\", \"unresolved_without\", possibly \"_case_nonce\" but we ignore.\n- Validate: case has these keys, items is list, required_failure_items and unresolved_without are lists, all items in these lists are in items? Not required but reasonable.\n- Check initial full configuration: all items. Compute outcome: if any item in unresolved_without not in full (should not), then raise? Actually full has all items, so no unresolved. Then check if all required_failure_items in full: must be true, else initial FAIL not satisfied. So raise ValueError if initial outcome is not FAIL.\n- Then implement ddmin algorithm as described.\n- Count tests: each oracle call counts.\n- At end, return dict.\n\nNeed to be careful about ordering: The subsets should be in order as they appear in the configuration list? The case items are in a specific order. The algorithm should respect that order. When partitioning, we split the list into contiguous chunks (order preserving). That is typical ddmin for ordered configurations. The problem says \"ordered configuration\". So we treat the items list as ordered. So partitioning splits the list into g contiguous segments (as evenly as possible). Testing subsets: the subset itself is a list in original order. Complements: the complement is the original list with that segment removed. That preserves order. Ties use source position: i.e., when multiple subsets or complements could reduce, we take the first one encountered? The spec says \"All ranking and traversal ties use the source position or identifier specified above\". So we should process subsets in order, and if a subset fails, we take that subset (first encountered). Similarly for complements. That is consistent with standard ddmin.\n\nAlso note: granularity can exceed length? We cap at len(config). When granularity > len(config), we stop? Actually algorithm loops while len(config) > 1 and granularity <= len(config). When granularity becomes larger than len(config), we can't partition into that many subsets, so we stop? But standard algorithm would increase granularity up to len(config) and then if no reduction, return config as minimal. So we set granularity = min(g*2, len(config)) after non-reduction. So when g == len(config), we partition into len(config) subsets each of size 1. Test each singleton; if any fails, that is reduction. If none, test complements (each of size len-1). If none, then g is already len, doubling would exceed, so we can set granularity = len (no change) and loop condition will still be true? Actually if g == len, we can still partition into len subsets. But after testing and no reduction, we double to min(2*len, len) = len, so no change, infinite loop. To avoid, we should break if no reduction when g == len. Standard ddmin stops when no reduction after testing all singletons and complements. So we can check if g == len(config) and no reduction, then break and return current config. That is typical: after all subsets of size 1 and complements of size len-1 have been tested without reduction, the set is 1-minimal.\n\nThus loop condition: while len(config) > 1:\n   partition into g subsets (g = min(g, len(config))). Actually we maintain g variable.\n   For each subset, test; if FAIL, set config = subset, g = 2, continue to next iteration.\n   For each complement, test; if FAIL, set config = complement, g = 2, continue.\n   If no reduction:\n       if g == len(config):\n           break  # minimal\n       else:\n           g = min(g*2, len(config))\n\nBut careful: when g == len(config), we already tested all singletons and complements. If no reduction, break.\n\nImplementation detail: We need to generate subsets correctly. Subset i from 0 to g-1: start index = i * len(config) // g, end index = (i+1)*len(config)//g. That yields contiguous chunks.\n\nNow, the test oracle function inside solve can be defined as inner function that takes list of items and returns \"PASS\", \"FAIL\", \"UNRESOLVED\". Use sets for membership check.\n\nCount tests: increment counter each time oracle is called. Include initial test of full configuration? The initial test is required to be FAIL, so we count it? The spec says \"test count\" probably includes all tests performed. We should count the initial test as well. So before loop, test full set, ensure FAIL, increment count. Then loop.\n\nAt end, test the final configuration? Actually the final configuration is the result of reduction; we have already tested it during the algorithm because it was the result of a subset or complement that failed, so it was tested. So final test count includes that. But after loop, we might have not tested the final config? Actually the loop ends when no reduction possible; the config at that point was already tested in the previous iteration (either as a subset that failed or as a complement that failed). If we break because g==len and no reduction, the config is the same as before the iteration, which was tested earlier. So we don't need extra test. However, ensure that if initial config is of length 1, we skip loop and return that with test count 1. That's fine.\n\nEdge cases: If initial config is length 1, it's already minimal, return it.\n\nNow, about output field names: \"one_minimal_failure\", \"test_count\", \"terminal_outcome\". The terminal outcome should be \"FAIL\". Also we need to include the nonce? Probably not. The spec says \"Return the 1-minimal failure, test count, and terminal outcome.\" That's it.\n\nImplement with validation: check that case dict has required keys. We'll raise ValueError with appropriate message if something invalid.\n\nNow produce the JSON response exactly as required: {\"implementation\": \"Python source code as a single string\"}.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. Use triple quotes? But JSON string must be escaped. We'll write the code as a string with proper escaping. Since we output JSON, we can use json.dumps on the python string to get proper escaping. But in this environment, we need to produce the raw JSON object. I'll manually escape.\n\nBetter: I'll write the code in a formatted way, then escape for JSON. But it's error-prone. I'll generate the Python source as a string with proper escapes using a programmatic approach in my mind. I'll produce a compact version without extra spaces to reduce escaping issues.\n\nI'll write the code as:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case['items']\n    required = case['required_failure_items']\n    unresolved = case['unresolved_without']\n    # validate\n    if not isinstance(items, list) or not isinstance(required, list) or not isinstance(unresolved, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid input types\")\n    # check initial full config\n    full = list(items)  # copy\n    def oracle(cfg):\n        # cfg is list of items\n        s = set(cfg)\n        if any(u not in s for u in unresolved):\n            return 'UNRESOLVED'\n        if all(r in s for r in required):\n            return 'FAIL'\n        return 'PASS'\n    test_count = 0\n    # initial test\n    outcome = oracle(full)\n    test_count += 1\n    if outcome != 'FAIL':\n        raise ValueError(\"Initial configuration does not produce FAIL\")\n    config = full\n    if len(config) == 1:\n        return {\"one_minimal_failure\": config, \"test_count\": test_count, \"terminal_outcome\": \"FAIL\"}\n    g = 2\n    while len(config) > 1:\n        n = len(config)\n        # partition into g subsets (contiguous)\n        subsets = []\n        for i in range(g):\n            start = i * n // g\n            end = (i+1) * n // g\n            subset = config[start:end]\n            subsets.append(subset)\n        found = False\n        # test subsets in order\n        for subset in subsets:\n            outcome = oracle(subset)\n            test_count += 1\n            if outcome == 'FAIL':\n                config = subset\n                g = 2\n                found = True\n                break\n        if found:\n            continue\n        # test complements\n        complements = []\n        for i, subset in enumerate(subsets):\n            # complement is config without that subset\n            # but we need to preserve order: concatenate left and right parts\n            left = config[: i*n//g]\n            right = config[(i+1)*n//g:]\n            comp = left + right\n            complements.append(comp)\n        for comp in complements:\n            outcome = oracle(comp)\n            test_count += 1\n            if outcome == 'FAIL':\n                config = comp\n                g = 2\n                found = True\n                break\n        if found:\n            continue\n        if g == n:\n            break\n        g = min(g*2, n)\n    # after loop, config is minimal\n    # ensure final config produces FAIL (should)\n    # but we already tested it, so no extra test\n    return {\"one_minimal_failure\": config, \"test_count\": test_count, \"terminal_outcome\": \"FAIL\"}\n\nBut note: The complement calculation uses start and end indices based on the same partition. That's correct.\n\nPotential issue: When we set config = subset after a subset fails, and config length might be 1, then loop condition len(config)>1 will exit. That's fine.\n\nNow, we need to ensure that we don't mutate input case. We make copies internally.\n\nAlso, handling of unresolved_without: the oracle returns UNRESOLVED if any unresolved item is missing. Note: If there are no unresolved items, then condition is never true, so only FAIL or PASS.\n\nNow, let's test with the given fixtures:\n\nCase 000: items d0..d7, required_failure=[d2,d3], unresolved_without=[]. Initial FAIL. Partition g=2: subsets: [d0,d1,d2,d3] and [d4,d5,d6,d7]. Test first subset: [d0,d1,d2,d3] contains both d2,d3 -> FAIL, so config reduces to that subset, g=2. Now n=4. Partition g=2: subsets [d0,d1] and [d2,d3]. Test subset [d0,d1] -> missing d2,d3 -> PASS. Test subset [d2,d3] -> contains both -> FAIL, config reduces to [d2,d3], g=2. n=2. Partition g=2: subsets [d2] and [d3]. Test [d2] -> missing d3 -> PASS. Test [d3] -> missing d2 -> PASS. Then test complements: complement of [d2] is [d3]; complement of [d3] is [d2]. Test [d3] -> PASS, [d2] -> PASS. No reduction. g==n=2, break. Return config [d2,d3] with test count: initial (1) + subsets in first iteration (2) + subsets second iteration (2) + subsets third iteration (2) + complements third iteration (2) = 1+2+2+2+2=9. But we also tested the initial config? Already counted. So final minimal is [d2,d3]. That seems correct.\n\nCase 001: items same, required=[d3,d4], unresolved_without=[d0]. Initial full config contains d0, so no unresolved, and all required present -> FAIL. Now algorithm: g=2 subsets: [d0,d1,d2,d3] and [d4,d5,d6,d7]. Test first subset: [d0,d1,d2,d3] contains d3 but missing d4 -> PASS (since required not all present). Test second subset: [d4,d5,d6,d7] contains d4 but missing d3 -> PASS. Then complements: complement of first subset is [d4,d5,d6,d7] (which we already tested as subset? Actually complement is second subset? Wait careful: complement of first subset is the second subset? Actually first subset indices 0-3, complement is indices 4-7 which we already tested as subset. So outcome same. complement of second subset is first subset. Both already tested. So no reduction. Then g doubles to 4. n=8. Partition into 4 subsets: [d0,d1], [d2,d3], [d4,d5], [d6,d7]. Test each subset: none contains both d3 and d4. So all PASS. Then complements: complement of [d0,d1] is [d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7] - that contains d3 and d4? Yes contains both, and also contains d0? It contains d0? No, d0 is removed, so that complement does not contain d0. Since unresolved_without includes d0, and d0 is missing, outcome should be UNRESOLVED. So test outcome = UNRESOLVED, not FAIL. So not reduction. Complement of [d2,d3] is [d0,d1,d4,d5,d6,d7] - contains d0, contains d3? No, d3 is removed, missing d3 so PASS. Complement of [d4,d5] is [d0,d1,d2,d3,d6,d7] - contains d0, contains d3,d4? Contains d3 and d4, so all r